  "_combination_note": "Combination rules fire on the INTERACTION of signals, not any single one -- the arbitrage that others miss. complexity_arbitrage answers two of the four questions at once: 'which bad property has a SOLVABLE problem' (a fixable constraint a capable buyer can clear) AND 'who is more likely to SELL' (a motivated/transitioning owner). A clean parcel with a motivated owner is a normal lead; a fixable-problem parcel with a motivated owner is one most buyers walk past -- which is exactly the edge. Emitted as a disposition-dimension lift; lower reliability since it's a derived inference, not a record.",
  "combination_rules": {
    "complexity_arbitrage": {
      "points": 10,
      "solvable_constraints": [
        "failed_septic", "septic_major_repair", "sewer_lateral", "brownfield", "ust_issue",
        "contaminated_site", "environmental", "fema_repetitive_loss", "co_hold_tco_expired",
        "unpermitted_work", "expired_stalled_permit", "stalled_subdivision", "stop_work_order",
        "foundation_complaint", "retaining_wall_failure", "unsafe_structure", "boarding_order",
        "failed_inspection"
      ],
      "motivation_dimensions": [
        "financial_distress", "ownership_transition", "landlord_fatigue", "disposition_probability"
      ]
    }
  },

  "_reliability_note": "Signal confidence (#15): reliability 1-5 per signal. Public-record signals are trustworthy (default 5); proxies/estimates and inferred/AI signals are lower. The score POINTS are not discounted (no double-counting); instead each lead gets a confidence 0-100 = points-weighted avg reliability, so a high score built on shaky signals is visible and filterable. Verified/deterministic signals stay at the default; only softer ones are overridden here.",
  "default_reliability": 5,
